==Early life, education, and teaching career==
Dunleavy was born and raised in [[Scranton, Pennsylvania]]. After graduating from [[Scranton High School (Pennsylvania)|Scranton Central High School]] in 1979, he earned a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in history at [[Misericordia University]] in 1983.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.pahomepage.com/news/alaska-governor-elect-is-nepa-native/1581805965|title=Alaska Governor-Elect is NEPA native|first=Mark|last=Hiller|date=November 9, 2018|website=Pahomepage.com|access-date=February 5, 2019|archive-date=March 27, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190327203414/https://www.pahomepage.com/news/alaska-governor-elect-is-nepa-native/1581805965|url-status=live}}</ref> In 1983, he moved to Alaska and his first job was at a logging camp in Southeast Alaska.<ref name="Biography of Mike Dunleavy">{{cite web |title=Biography of Mike Dunleavy |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/meet-mike-dunleavy/ |access-date=12 January 2022 |website=State of Alaska |archive-date=January 7, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220107061025/https://gov.alaska.gov/home/meet-mike-dunleavy/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Later, Dunleavy earned his [[master's degree]] in [[education]] from the [[University of Alaska Fairbanks]].<ref name="PVS">{{cite web |title=Senator Mike Dunleavy's Biography |url=http://votesmart.org/candidate/biography/140395 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220717052133/https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/140395/michael-dunleavy |archive-date=July 17, 2022 |access-date=October 30, 2013 |publisher=[[Project Vote Smart]]}}</ref> He spent nearly two decades in northwest Arctic communities working as a teacher, principal, and superintendent. Dunleavy's wife, Rose, is from the [[Kobuk River|Kobuk River Valley]] community of [[Noorvik, Alaska|Noorvik]]. They have three children, who were raised in both rural and urban Alaska. In 2004, Dunleavy and his family moved to [[Wasilla, Alaska|Wasilla]], where he owned an educational consulting firm and worked on a number of  statewide educational projects. Before his election to the [[Alaska Senate]], Dunleavy served on the board of the [[Matanuska-Susitna Borough School District]], including two years as its president.<ref name="Biography of Mike Dunleavy"/>

==State legislative career==
Dunleavy defeated incumbent state senator [[Linda Menard]] (redistricted from District G) in the District D August 28, 2012, Republican primary with 2,802 votes (57.42%).<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/12PRIM/data/results.htm |title= State of Alaska 2012 Primary Election August 28, 2012 Official Results |publisher= State of Alaska Division of Elections |location= Juneau, Alaska |access-date= October 30, 2013 |archive-date= December 30, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131230023533/http://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/12PRIM/data/results.htm |url-status= live }}</ref> He was unopposed in the November 6 general election and won with 11,724 votes (94.24%) against [[write-in candidate]]s.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/12GENR/data/results.htm |title= State of Alaska 2012 General Election November 6, 2012 Official Results |publisher= State of Alaska Division of Elections |location= Juneau, Alaska |access-date= October 30, 2013 |archive-date= February 24, 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190224162440/http://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/12GENR/data/results.htm |url-status= live }}</ref>

Dunleavy represented District D from 2013 to 2014, before [[redistricting]] placed him in District E, where he continued to serve until resigning in January 2018 to run for governor.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Mike Dunleavy |url= https://ballotpedia.org/Mike_Dunleavy |access-date= 2025-06-18 |website= Ballotpedia |language= en}}</ref>

Dunleavy held various committee positions during his first term. He served as chair of the Senate Labor & Commerce Committee, co-chair of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System (TAPS) Throughput Special Committee, vice-chair of the Senate Education Committee, the Senate Finance Committee, and a member of the Legislative Council.<ref>{{cite web |title= Senator Mike Dunleavy Committee Membership |url= https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Member/Detail/28?code=DNL#tab7_2 |website= Alaska State Legislature |access-date= 29 May 2024}}</ref>

Dunleavy also chaired the Senate Environmental Conservation Finance Subcommittee and the Fish & Game Finance Subcommittee, and served on Legislative Budget & Audit and World Trade committees in his first term.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Alaska State Legislature |url= https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Member/Detail/28?code=DNL&utm#tab7_2 |access-date= 2025-06-18 |website= Alaska State Legislature}}</ref>

As co‑chair of the Trans‑Alaska Pipeline System (TAPS) Throughput Special Committee, Dunleavy oversaw hearings on oil and gas bills such as SB 21 (a [[ConocoPhillips]]-supported oil tax credit bill).<ref>{{Cite web |title= ConocoPhillips employees steer Alaska oil tax cut bill through Legislature |url= https://www.adn.com/politics/article/series-conoco-employees-help-ease-oil-tax-bill-through-legislature/2013/03/28/ |access-date=2025-06-18 |website=Anchorage Daily News}}</ref>

In 2014, Dunleavy ran unopposed in the Republican primary election on August 19.<ref>{{cite web |title= 2014 State Primary Elections - Alaska |url= https://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/14PRIM/data/results.htm |website=Alaska Division of Elections |access-date= 31 May 2024}}</ref> He defeated Democratic nominee Warren Keogh in the November general election with 64.65% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |title= 2014 General Elections - Official Results |url= https://www.elections.alaska.gov/results/14GENR/data/results.htm |website= Alaska Division of Elections}}</ref>

During his second term, Dunleavy chaired the Education Committee and was a member of the Finance Committee and the Special Committee on Federal Overreach.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Member/Detail/28?code=DNL#undefined |website= Senator Mike Dunleavy Committee Membership |access-date= 31 May 2024 |title= Alaska State Legislature }}</ref>

Dunleavy introduced multiple bills and resolutions to expand and enhance public charter schools, correspondence study programs, tax credits for educational contributions, and a constitutional amendment for the use of public education. He was successful with legislation in his second term, establishing Alaska's parental bill of rights.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/get_documents.asp?session=29&docid=66509 |website=Summary of Senate Changes to House Bill 156 |access-date=4 June 2024 |title=Alaska State Legislature}}</ref>

In 2013, Dunleavy sponsored legislation to create Alaska's correspondence school allotment program, SB100.<ref>{{cite web |title=Alaska State Legislature |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Bill/Detail/28?Root=SB%20100#tab6 |website=www.akleg.gov |access-date=1 December 2025}}</ref>{{Primary source inline|plural=yes|date=December 2025}} This program allowed parents of students enrolled in correspondence (homeschool) programs to use state education funds, called "allotments", for educational materials and services from public, private, or religious organizations. The legislation was passed in 2014 via an omnibus education package, HB278.<ref>{{cite web |title=Alaska State Legislature |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Bill/Detail/28?Root=HB%20278 |website=www.akleg.gov |access-date=1 December 2025}}</ref>{{Primary source inline|plural=yes|date=December 2025}} Dunleavy's initiative aimed to increase educational flexibility for families, enabling them to tailor their children's education to better meet individual needs.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2014-04-23 |title=Legislative negotiators seek education resolution |url=https://www.peninsulaclarion.com/news/legislative-negotiators-seek-education-resolution/ |access-date=2024-07-09 |website=Peninsula Clarion |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Governor of Alaska==

===Elections===

==== 2018 ====
{{main|2018 Alaska gubernatorial election}}
In 2017, Dunleavy announced he would run for governor in 2018 but abandoned the race in September 2017, citing heart problems.<ref>[https://www.adn.com/politics/2017/09/12/dunleavy-suspends-campaign-for-alaska-governor-as-huggins-files-to-run/ Dunleavy suspends campaign for Alaska governor as Huggins files to run] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116214501/https://www.adn.com/politics/2017/09/12/dunleavy-suspends-campaign-for-alaska-governor-as-huggins-files-to-run/ |date=November 16, 2018 }}, ''[[Alaska Dispatch News]]'', Nathaniel Herz, September 12, 2017. Retrieved September 13, 2017.</ref> In December 2017 he announced his return to the race.<ref>[http://www.frontiersman.com/news/dunleavy-back-in-race-for-governor/article_9c111184-e72d-11e7-98f4-fb5076d84260.html Denleavy back in race for governor] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180114181116/http://www.frontiersman.com/news/dunleavy-back-in-race-for-governor/article_9c111184-e72d-11e7-98f4-fb5076d84260.html |date=January 14, 2018 }}, ''[[Mat-Su Valley Frontiersman]]'', December 22, 2017.</ref> He resigned his Senate seat effective January 15, 2018, to focus on his campaign.<ref>[http://juneauempire.com/state/news/alaska-legislature/2018-01-08/state-senator-mike-dunleavy-resigns-legislature-boost State senator Mike Dunleavy resigns from Legislature to boost gubernatorial run] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180226085353/http://juneauempire.com/state/news/alaska-legislature/2018-01-08/state-senator-mike-dunleavy-resigns-legislature-boost |date=February 26, 2018 }}, ''[[Juneau Empire]]'', James Brooks, January 9, 2018.</ref> Retired [[United States Air Force]] lieutenant colonel [[Mike Shower]] was chosen as his successor by Governor [[Bill Walker (U.S. politician)|Bill Walker]] and confirmed by the Alaska Senate caucus after numerous replacement candidates were rejected.<ref>[https://www.alaskapublic.org/2018/02/22/mike-shower-confirmed-to-dunleavy-senate-seat/ Mike Shower confirmed to Dunleavy Senate seat] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180227034735/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2018/02/22/mike-shower-confirmed-to-dunleavy-senate-seat/ |date=February 27, 2018 }}, ''[[Alaska Public Media]]'', Phillip Manning, February 22, 2018. Retrieved February 26, 2018.</ref>

==== 2022 ====
{{main|2022 Alaska gubernatorial election}}
In August 2021, Dunleavy announced his candidacy for reelection in 2022.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Dunleavy for Governor|url=https://www.dunleavygovernor.com/|access-date=2021-10-27|website=www.dunleavygovernor.com|archive-date=October 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027015717/https://www.dunleavygovernor.com/|url-status=live}}</ref> He was reelected with 50.3% of the vote, becoming the first incumbent Republican governor of Alaska to be reelected since Jay Hammond in [[1978 Alaska gubernatorial election|1978]] and the first Alaska governor of any political affiliation to be reelected since Tony Knowles in [[1998 Alaska gubernatorial election|1998]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Alaska Election Results 2022: Live Map {{!}} Midterm Races by County & District |url=https://www.politico.com/2022-election/results/alaska/ |access-date=2022-12-12 |website=www.politico.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url = https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-business-alaska-bill-walker-acfca581940b7d4045df3f2e13f1538d|title = Mike Dunleavy is 1st Alaska governor reelected since '98|last = Bohrer|first = Becky|date = November 25, 2022|access-date = November 25, 2022|work = [[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
[[File:Governor Mike Dunleavy. Juneau, Alaska.jpg|thumb|right|Dunleavy speaking in the governor's office, accompanied by members of his administration, on May 15, 2019.]]
[[File:2019 08 25-13.37.45.682-CDT.jpg|thumb|Dunleavy meeting with residents at a meeting regarding the [[Deshka Landing Fire]] in 2019.]]
[[File:Gyeonggi Province Governor Kim Dong-yeon meets with Alaska Governor Mike J. Dunleavy in Seoul 20250325 (02).jpg|thumb|Dunleavy meeting with South Korea's [[Gyeonggi Province]] Governor [[Kim Dong-yeon (politician)|Kim Dong-yeon]] in March 2025.]]
Dunleavy and [[Kevin Meyer (politician)|Kevin Meyer]] were the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominees for governor and lieutenant governor of Alaska, respectively, and were elected in the November 2018 general election. Dunleavy was sworn in on December 3, 2018. He appointed [[Kevin Clarkson]] to be [[Alaska Attorney General|Alaska attorney general]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2018/12/05/dunleavy-announces-alaska-attorney-general-public-safety-and-prison-appointments/|title=Dunleavy attorney general appointee Kevin Clarkson is lawyer with ties to religious-liberty causes|date=December 5, 2018|newspaper=[[Anchorage Daily News]]|access-date=February 5, 2019|archive-date=December 28, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181228083311/https://www.adn.com/politics/2018/12/05/dunleavy-announces-alaska-attorney-general-public-safety-and-prison-appointments/|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2021, Lieutenant Governor Kevin Meyer announced that he would not seek reelection. Dunleavy picked [[Nancy Dahlstrom]] as his running mate in the 2022 election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=George |first=Kavitha |date=2022-12-13 |title=Nancy Dahlstrom has spent most of her 20-year political career out of the spotlight. Now she's second in command. |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/2022-12-13/alaskas-new-lieutenant-governor-says-shes-ready-to-head-state-elections |access-date=2026-06-17 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en}}</ref>

====Natural disasters and response====
=====2018 earthquake=====
A massive [[2018 Anchorage earthquake|7.1 magnitude earthquake]] struck Southcentral Alaska on November 30, 2018, three days before Dunleavy took office, causing significant damage throughout the region.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Domonoske |first1=Camila |title=Magnitude 7.0 Earthquake Shakes Alaska, Damaging Roads, Buildings |url=https://www.npr.org/2018/11/30/672240051/magnitude-7-0-earthquake-shakes-alaska-tsunami-warning-in-effect |access-date=4 June 2024 |agency=NPR News |date=November 30, 2018}}</ref> Within hours, he and his team assessed the situation at the State Emergency Operations Center (SEOC) alongside the outgoing administration of Governor [[Bill Walker (American politician)|Bill Walker]]. On December 31, Dunleavy returned to the State Emergency Operations Center to personally thank the SEOC staff for their service.<ref>{{cite web |title=Governor Dunleavy Thanks Emergency Operations Center Staff |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-thanks-emergency-operations-center-staff/ |website=State of Alaska |date=January 2019 |access-date=4 June 2024}}</ref>

On January 3, 2019, Dunleavy requested a Presidential Major Disaster Declaration<ref>{{cite web |last1=Polk |first1=Leroy |title=Gov. Dunleavy requests disaster relief for Nov. 30 earthquake damage |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/content/news/Gov-Mike-Dunleavy-requests-disaster-relief-for-Alaska-earthquake-damage-503855921.html |website=Alaska's News Source |date=January 3, 2019 |publisher=Gray Media Group |access-date=4 June 2024}}</ref> for federal assistance to Alaska. He thanked President [[Donald Trump]] for approving the request for disaster assistance on January 31.<ref>{{cite news |title=Governor Dunleavy Welcomes Presidential Disaster Declaration |url=https://alaska-native-news.com/governor-dunleavy-welcomes-presidential-disaster-declaration/39959/ |access-date=4 June 2024 |agency=Alaska Native News |date=2 May 2019}}</ref>
Dunleavy sponsored the Disaster Relief Act of 2019<ref>{{cite web |title=SB38 |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Bill/Detail/31?Root=SB%20%2038 |website=Alaska State Legislature |access-date=6 June 2024}}</ref> during the legislative session to assist with response, recovery, and relief efforts after the earthquake. The bill passed the legislature and was signed into law on April 5, 2019.<ref>{{cite news |title=Alaska Legislature passes disaster relief bill |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2019/mar/29/alaska-legislature-passes-disaster-relief-bill/ |access-date=6 June 2024 |work=The Washington Times |agency=Associated Press |date=March 19, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Brooks |first1=James |title=Dunleavy signs first bill, sending millions to Alaska earthquake reconstruction effort |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2019/04/08/dunleavy-signs-first-bill-sending-millions-to-alaska-earthquake-reconstruction-effort/ |access-date=6 June 2024 |work=Anchorage Daily News |date=April 8, 2019}}</ref> Dunleavy said: "This bill is going to be very important for Alaska – Southcentral Alaska – to make sure we get our bridges and roads up to speed. As springtime continues to uncover potential infrastructure issues, this relief money is going to help tremendously."<ref>{{cite web |title=Governor Signs Disaster Relief Bill Into Law |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-signs-disaster-relief-bill-into-law/ |website=State of Alaska |date=April 6, 2019 |access-date=6 June 2024}}</ref>

===== Wildfires =====
In August 2019, Dunleavy issued a disaster declaration as a result of multiple wildfires in Alaska.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Law |first=Tara |date=2019-08-21 |title=About 2.5 Million Acres in Alaska Have Burned. The State's Fire Seasons Are Getting Worse, Experts Say |url=https://time.com/5657188/alaska-fires-long-climate-change/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |magazine=TIME |language=en}}</ref> 2.6 million acres burned that summer. "This declaration frees up financial assistance to help the victims of these devastating fires begin to rebuild their lives as quickly as possible", he said.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Osborne |first=Natalie |date=2019-08-24 |title=Governor Dunleavy Issues Disaster Declaration for Wildfires |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-issues-disaster-declaration-for-wildfires/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref>

===== Typhoon Halong =====
In October 2025, the remnants of [[Typhoon Halong (2025)|Typhoon Halong]] struck Western Alaska as a powerful [[extratropical cyclone]], bringing hurricane-force winds and record [[storm surge]] to the [[Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta]]. The storm killed one woman in [[Kwigillingok, Alaska|Kwigillingok]], left two people missing, and displaced more than 1,500 residents, with the predominantly [[Yup'ik]] villages of [[Kipnuk, Alaska|Kipnuk]] and Kwigillingok hardest hit.<ref name="npr-halong">{{cite news |title=What we know about the devastating storm in Western Alaska |publisher=NPR |date=October 17, 2025 |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/10/17/nx-s1-5577974/typhoon-halong-western-alaska-villages-damaged-yukon-kuskokwim-delta |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref>

Dunleavy issued a state disaster declaration on October 9 for areas threatened by the storms, and amended it as conditions worsened to encompass the affected areas of the Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta.<ref name="lynnwood">{{cite news |title=Catastrophic flooding from typhoon Halong devastates Alaska native communities |work=Lynnwood Times |date=October 20, 2025 |url=https://lynnwoodtimes.com/2025/10/20/alaska-native/ |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> "Every effort will be made to help those hit by this storm", he said.<ref name="akpublic-damage">{{cite news |title=Remnants of Typhoon Halong bring widespread damage to Western Alaska |work=Alaska Public Media |date=October 12, 2025 |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/public-safety/2025-10-12/live-updates-a-major-storm-is-bringing-dangerous-flooding-and-hurricane-force-winds-to-western-alaska |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> Under his declaration, the [[State Emergency Operations Center]] was elevated to its highest response level, and Dunleavy directed the [[Alaska National Guard]] and the [[Alaska State Defense Force|Alaska Organized Militia]] to support search-and-rescue and evacuation operations, which moved residents from flooded villages to [[Bethel, Alaska|Bethel]] and then to [[Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage]] in what state officials called the largest mass evacuation in Alaska history.<ref name="stripes">{{cite news |title=Alaska National Guard participates in one of the largest evacuations in the state's history |work=Stars and Stripes |date=October 16, 2025 |url=https://www.stripes.com/theaters/us/2025-10-16/alaska-national-guard-historic-airlift-typhoon-19448586.html |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref><ref name="beacon-stories">{{cite news |title=Top Alaska public safety stories of 2025: Typhoon Halong, crime and immigration enforcement |work=Alaska Beacon |date=December 31, 2025 |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2025/12/31/top-alaska-public-safety-stories-of-2025-typhoon-halong-crime-and-immigration-enforcement/ |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref>

At a news conference in Anchorage on October 13, Dunleavy pledged sustained assistance, saying the state would "continue to help, not just today, tomorrow, but weeks and months on".<ref name="akpublic-devastated">{{cite news |title=Officials say storm 'completely devastated' Western Alaska communities |work=Alaska Public Media |date=October 13, 2025 |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/public-safety/2025-10-13/officials-say-storm-completely-devastated-western-alaska-communities |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> He toured Kipnuk and Kwigillingok on October 17, calling Kipnuk "a disaster area" where homes were "tossed everywhere".<ref name="abc">{{cite news |title=Hundreds evacuated, homes destroyed as Alaska communities reel from devastating flooding |publisher=ABC News |date=October 18, 2025 |url=https://abcnews.com/US/hundreds-evacuated-homes-destroyed-alaska-communities-reel-devastating/story?id=126612663 |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref>

On October 16, Dunleavy submitted a request to President Trump for a [[Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act|Presidential Major Disaster Declaration]] covering both the Yukon–Kuskokwim region and storm-affected areas of northwestern Alaska, saying that many residents would not be able to return to their communities for more than 18 months.<ref name="abc"/><ref name="beacon-future">{{cite news |title=Typhoon disaster in Western Alaska raises questions around the region's future |work=Alaska Beacon |date=October 17, 2025 |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2025/10/17/typhoon-disaster-in-western-alaska-raises-questions-around-the-regions-future/ |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> Trump approved the declaration on October 22, announcing $25 million in federal aid; Dunleavy said it authorized a 100% federal cost share for all categories of assistance for an initial 90 days.<ref name="newssource-decl">{{cite news |title=President Donald Trump signs major disaster declaration from former Typhoon Halong |work=Alaska's News Source |date=October 22, 2025 |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/10/22/house-speaker-governor-mike-dunleavy-dc-amid-pending-presidential-disaster-declaration/ |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> He called the declaration "instrumental for ongoing response and recovery efforts" and thanked Trump and his administration.<ref name="newssource-decl"/> In early November, with repair and debris-removal work paused for winter, Dunleavy signed a 30-day extension of the state declaration, with the recovery effort set to resume in spring 2026 in coordination with federal agencies, tribal organizations, and local partners.<ref name="extension">{{cite news |title=Governor extends disaster declaration for Halong's impacts |work=Alaska's News Source |publisher=KTUU |date=November 8, 2025 |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/11/08/governor-extends-disaster-declaration-halongs-impacts/ |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref>

==== Relationship with legislature ====
During Dunleavy's first six years as governor, at least one house of the [[Alaska Legislature]] had been led by Republicans. But during the [[34th Alaska State Legislature|34th legislature]], when both houses were controlled by a Democratic-led [[bipartisan coalition]], Dunleavy vetoed more than one quarter of the bills the legislature passed, breaking the state record for most vetoes in a single legislative term.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Brooks |first= James |date= 2026-07-13 |title= Alaska Gov. Mike Dunleavy sets state record for most vetoes in a single Legislature |url= https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/alaska-legislature/2026-07-13/alaska-gov-mike-dunleavy-sets-state-record-for-most-vetoes-in-a-single-legislature |access-date= 2026-07-14 |website= Alaska Public Media |language= en}}</ref>

==== Budget ====
On June 28, 2019, Dunleavy exercised [[line-item veto]] authority as governor to make cuts of $433 million, including a cut of $130 million (41%) of state contributions to the University of Alaska.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/education/2019/06/28/dunleavy-veto-is-devastating-says-university-of-alaska-president/ |title= University of Alaska president: Dunleavy veto is unprecedented and 'devastating'|date= 2019-06-28| website= Anchorage Daily News| language= en-US|access-date= 2019-06-29|archive-date= June 29, 2019| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190629035848/https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/education/2019/06/28/dunleavy-veto-is-devastating-says-university-of-alaska-president/ |url-status= live}}</ref> He also vetoed $335,000 from the budget of the [[Alaska Supreme Court]] on the grounds that the Court had held that the state was constitutionally required to fund elective abortions.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.alaskapublic.org/2019/06/28/alaska-gov-dunleavy-wields-veto-pen-to-attack-state-supreme-court-over-abortion-ruling/ |title= Alaska Gov. Dunleavy wields veto pen to attack state Supreme Court over abortion ruling| last1= Herz|first1= Nathaniel|date= 2019-06-29| website= Alaska Public Media| language= en-US |access-date= 2019-07-01| archive-date= July 1, 2019| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190701095404/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2019/06/28/alaska-gov-dunleavy-wields-veto-pen-to-attack-state-supreme-court-over-abortion-ruling/ |url-status= live}}</ref>

In September 2020, Dunleavy agreed to reimburse the state $2,800 for allegedly partisan advertisements that were paid for with state funds. He did not admit wrongdoing, but said it was the state's best interest to resolve the allegations.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/alaska/articles/2020-09-08/dunleavy-to-pay-2-800-after-ads-found-to-violate-ethics-law |title= Dunleavy to Pay $2,800 After Ads Found to Violate Ethics Law |date= September 8, 2020 |website= www.usnews.com |access-date= 2021-01-30 |archive-date= February 4, 2021 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210204023257/https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/alaska/articles/2020-09-08/dunleavy-to-pay-2-800-after-ads-found-to-violate-ethics-law |url-status= live}}</ref> On September 6, 2022, a complaint was filed alleging that Dunleavy's campaign was paying staffers with state funds.<ref>[https://alaskapublic.org/2022/09/07/dunleavy-accused-of-using-state-funds-to-pay-campaign-staffers-and-violating-campaign-finance-rules/ Dunleavy accused of using state funds to pay campaign staffers and violating campaign finance rules], ''[[Alaska Public Media]]'', Kavitha George, September 7, 2022. Retrieved September 7, 2022.</ref>

In 2023, one of Dunleavy's advisors, Jeremy Cubas, resigned after inflammatory statements he made on his podcast became common knowledge.<ref name=Cubas>{{Cite web|url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2023/05/31/alaska-gov-dunleavys-policy-adviser-who-said-divorce-is-worse-than-rape-resigns/|title=Alaska Gov. Dunleavy's policy adviser who said 'divorce is worse than rape' resigns|website=Anchorage Daily News}}</ref>

==== Job approval ====
[[File:Governor_Dunleavy's_State_of_the_State_2020.jpg|thumb|Governor Mike Dunleavy's State of the State before the Alaska State Legislature in 2020]]
A 2021 Morning Consult poll listed Dunleavy among the top 16 most popular governors in the United States.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Writer |first=Andrew Stanton Weekend Staff |date=2021-11-20 |title=14 of the 16 Most Popular Governors Are Republicans |url=https://www.newsweek.com/14-16-most-popular-governors-are-republicans-1651599 |access-date=2024-06-07 |website=Newsweek |language=en}}</ref>

In 2023, a Morning Consult poll showed Dunleavy as the fifth-most popular governor, with a job approval rating of 63%.<ref>{{Cite web |last=MultiState |title=Decoding 2023 Gubernatorial Approval Ratings |url=https://www.multistate.us/insider/2023/8/4/decoding-2023-gubernatorial-approval-ratings |access-date=2024-06-07 |website=MultiState |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Recall attempt ====
On July 15, 2019, an effort to recall Dunleavy began after a public backlash over his cuts to public assistance, education and the University of Alaska ($135 million cut to state funding, about a 41% reduction).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/0677d449905b90d4d47df56464013222|title=Alaska university head offers positive outlook in address|date=2020-03-02|website=AP NEWS|access-date=2020-03-02|archive-date=March 2, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200302162500/https://apnews.com/0677d449905b90d4d47df56464013222|url-status=live}}</ref> It was the second recall petition against a governor in Alaska history, the first being the failed petition against Governor [[Wally Hickel]].<ref name="Recall History">{{cite web |title=Recall History |url=https://www.elections.alaska.gov/doc/forms/H54.pdf |website=elections.alaska.gov |publisher=Alaska Division of Elections |access-date=April 8, 2021 |date=August 29, 2019}}</ref> Had the recall election been successful, [[Lieutenant Governor of Alaska|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Kevin Meyer (politician)|Kevin Meyer]] would have become governor.<ref name="ballotpedia">{{cite web |title=Mike Dunleavy recall, Governor of Alaska (2019-2021) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Mike_Dunleavy_recall,_Governor_of_Alaska_(2019-2021) |website=ballotpedia.org |publisher=Ballotpedia |access-date=April 8, 2021}}</ref>

To have the petition certified by the Division of Elections, the petitioners were first required to submit 28,501 signatures (approximately 10% of the voting population in Alaska's last general election).<ref>{{cite web |last1=Minemyer |first1=Derek |title=Alaskans mount effort to recall governor as huge budget cuts threaten education, Medicaid |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/alaskans-mount-effort-recall-governor-huge-budget-cuts-threaten-education-n1040951 |date=August 12, 2019 |website=nbcnews.com |publisher=NBC News |access-date=August 12, 2019 |archive-date=August 12, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190812221130/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/alaskans-mount-effort-recall-governor-huge-budget-cuts-threaten-education-n1040951 |url-status=live }}</ref> On September 5, 2019, volunteers submitted 49,006 petition signatures.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2019/09/05/recall-dunleavy-campaign-turns-in-48000-signatures-collected-in-five-weeks/|title=Recall Dunleavy campaign turns in 49,000 signatures collected in 5 weeks|date=2019-09-05|website=Anchorage Daily News|language=en-US|access-date=2019-09-07|archive-date=September 9, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190909030625/https://www.adn.com/politics/2019/09/05/recall-dunleavy-campaign-turns-in-48000-signatures-collected-in-five-weeks/|url-status=live}}</ref> On November 4, 2019, the Division of Elections declined to certify the recall petition after the Alaska attorney general [[Kevin Clarkson]], a Dunleavy appointee, issued a legal opinion.<ref>[https://www.adn.com/politics/2018/12/05/dunleavy-announces-alaska-attorney-general-public-safety-and-prison-appointments/ Attorney general appointee Kevin Clarkson is lawyer with ties to religious-liberty causes], ''[[Anchorage Daily News]]'', James Brooks, December 5, 2018. Retrieved September 7, 2022.</ref> Clarkson acknowledged that the petitioners had submitted enough signatures and paid the necessary fees, but asserted that "the four allegations against the governor 'fail to meet any of the listed grounds for recall—neglect of duty, incompetence, or lack of fitness'". The petitioners said they would appeal the division's decision.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2019/11/04/alaska-division-of-elections-rejects-recall-of-gov-mike-dunleavy/|title=Alaska Division of Elections rejects recall petition for Gov. Dunleavy|date=November 4, 2019|website=Anchorage Daily News|access-date=November 6, 2019|archive-date=November 6, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191106073113/https://www.adn.com/politics/2019/11/04/alaska-division-of-elections-rejects-recall-of-gov-mike-dunleavy/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In January 2020, Anchorage Superior Court Judge Eric Aarseth rejected the division's decision not to certify the recall petition. The state appealed Aarseth's ruling to the Alaska Supreme Court, which on May 8 affirmed that the recall effort could proceed.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Beran |first1=Jaclyn |title=Alaska Supreme Court rules Gov. Mike Dunleavy recall can proceed |url=https://news.ballotpedia.org/2020/05/12/alaska-supreme-court-rules-gov-mike-dunleavy-recall-can-proceed/ |website=Ballotpedia News |access-date=28 May 2020 |date=12 May 2020 |archive-date=May 29, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200529204709/https://news.ballotpedia.org/2020/05/12/alaska-supreme-court-rules-gov-mike-dunleavy-recall-can-proceed/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Bohrer |first1=Becky |title=Alaska Supreme Court: Recall effort can proceed |url=https://www.juneauempire.com/news/alaska-supreme-court-recall-effort-can-proceed/ |website=Juneau Empire |access-date=28 May 2020 |date=8 May 2020 |archive-date=May 18, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200518152914/https://www.juneauempire.com/news/alaska-supreme-court-recall-effort-can-proceed/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The "Recall Dunleavy" effort failed to submit enough signatures to trigger a recall election in November 2020 or in 2021.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2020/08/04/recall-dunleavy-effort-misses-deadline-to-appear-in-general-election/ |title=Recall Dunleavy effort misses deadline to appear in general election - Alaska Public Media |publisher=Alaskapublic.org |date=2020-08-04 |access-date=2021-01-30 |archive-date=September 30, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930190316/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2020/08/04/recall-dunleavy-effort-misses-deadline-to-appear-in-general-election/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Dunleavy won a second term as governor in [[2022 Alaska gubernatorial election|November 2022]] as part of the usual electoral process, making him the first governor to be reelected since [[Tony Knowles (politician)|Tony Knowles]] in [[1998 Alaska gubernatorial election|1998]], and the first Republican governor to be reelected since [[Jay Hammond]] in [[1978 Alaska gubernatorial election|1978]].

== Foreign trade and international affairs ==
=== Alaska LNG and the China negotiations ===
When Dunleavy took office in December 2018, the [[Alaska Gasline Development Corporation]] (AGDC), a state corporation created to advance the proposed [[Alaska LNG]] Project, was in negotiations with a Chinese state-owned consortium. In November 2017, under Governor [[Bill Walker]], AGDC had signed a nonbinding joint development agreement in Beijing with [[Sinopec]], [[Bank of China]], and CIC Capital (an investment arm of the [[China Investment Corporation]]), in the presence of President Trump and Chinese President [[Xi Jinping]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Alaska, China sign natural gas pipeline project deal |work=CBS News |date=November 9, 2017 |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/alaska-signs-gas-pipeline-project-deal-with-china/ |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Bradner |first=Tim |title=Alaska Delegation Welcomes Joint Development Agreement to Advance Alaska LNG Project |publisher=U.S. Senate Committee on Energy and Natural Resources |date=November 9, 2017 |url=https://www.energy.senate.gov/2017/11/alaska-delegation-welcomes-joint-development-agreement |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref> 

After taking office, Dunleavy did not pursue the Chinese framework. AGDC instead sought partnerships directly with North Slope producers and other international buyers, and the joint development agreement was not extended. AGDC later conducted a public solicitation for a new lead developer and on January 6, 2025, announced an exclusive framework agreement to privately develop the project; the developer, initially unnamed, was confirmed within days to be [[Glenfarne Group]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Alaska's LNG project moves closer to reality after 10 years in the making, with Glenfarne as private investor |work=Offshore Energy |date=January 21, 2025 |url=https://www.offshore-energy.biz/alaskas-lng-project-moves-closer-to-reality-after-10-years-in-the-making-with-glenfarne-as-private-investor/ |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref> AGDC and Glenfarne signed binding definitive agreements on March 27, 2025, under which Glenfarne became majority owner and lead developer, acquiring a 75% stake in 8 Star Alaska, the AGDC subsidiary holding the project's assets, while the state retained 25%.<ref>{{cite web |title=Glenfarne Becomes Lead Developer for the Alaska LNG Project |publisher=Glenfarne Group |date=March 27, 2025 |url=https://glenfarnegroup.com/glenfarne-becomes-lead-developer-for-the-alaska-lng-project/ |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref>

=== Trade mission to Japan ===
[[File:Governor Michael J. Dunleavy Trade Mission to Japan 2019.jpg|left|thumb|Alaska Governor Michael J. Dunleavy pictured here with representatives of ConocoPhillips, Mitsubishi, TOGAS, and JERA, the original Alaska LNG Project partners involved with the first shipment of Alaska LNG to Japan.]]
Dunleavy's first trade mission as governor was to Japan in November 2019, timed to coincide with the 50th anniversary of the first Alaska LNG shipment, which had been delivered from the [[Kenai, Alaska|Kenai]] LNG terminal to the Negishi LNG Receiving Terminal near Tokyo in November 1969.<ref>{{cite web |title=A Look Back: Governor Dunleavy and First Lady's Trade Mission to Japan |publisher=Office of the Governor of Alaska |date=November 8, 2019 |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/%F0%9F%93%B7-a-look-back-governor-dunleavy-and-first-ladys-trade-mission-to-japan/ |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=50th Year Anniversary of LNG (liquefied natural gas) to Japan |publisher=JERA |date=November 1, 2019 |url=https://www.jera.co.jp/english/information/20191101_419 |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref> On November 6, Dunleavy toured the Negishi terminal and met with executives from [[Tokyo Gas]].<ref>{{cite web |title=A Look Back: Governor Dunleavy and First Lady's Trade Mission to Japan |publisher=Office of the Governor of Alaska |date=November 8, 2019 |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/%F0%9F%93%B7-a-look-back-governor-dunleavy-and-first-ladys-trade-mission-to-japan/ |access-date=June 17, 2026}}</ref>

== Political positions ==

=== Economy and workforce development ===
In 2020, Dunleavy established the Alaska Development Team to promote business growth and attract new investments to the state. The team aims to facilitate business operations in Alaska and encourage companies to invest in industries like oil and gas, mining, tourism, commercial fishing, and aerospace.

In 2023, Dunleavy issued Administrative Order 343 to address workforce shortages by removing four-year degree requirements for most state jobs. This initiative aimed to expand employment opportunities and increase workforce flexibility. "If we're going to address our labor shortage, we have to recognize the value that apprenticeships, on-the-job training, military training, trade schools and other experience provides applicants. If a person can do the job, we shouldn't be holding anyone back just because they don't have a degree."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Klecka |first=Joey |last2=Symmes |first2=Elena |date=2023-02-14 |title=4-year degree no longer required for some state jobs |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2023/02/14/4-year-degree-no-longer-required-some-state-jobs/ |access-date=2024-10-01 |website=Alaska News Source |language=en}}</ref>

==== Alaska LNG Project ====
{{Main|Alaska LNG}}
[[File:Alaska oil and natural gas pipelines.webp|thumb|Alaska oil and natural gas pipelines, and proposed natural gas pipelines.]]
Dunleavy's administration has made significant efforts to enhance Alaska's economy and workforce development. One major initiative is the advancement of the [[Alaska LNG|Alaska LNG Project]], which aims to build an 800-mile pipeline to transport [[liquefied natural gas]] (LNG) from the [[North Slope Borough, Alaska|North Slope]] to southern Alaska for export, with potential markets in Asia. "The Alaska LNG project is well positioned for Alaska to realize the decades-old dream of bringing our natural gas off the North Slope for the benefit of Alaskans and worldwide markets", Dunleavy said.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-02-01 |title=Governor Dunleavy Highlights Competitive Strength of AK LNG in Report |url=https://alaska-native-news.com/governor-dunleavy-highlights-competitive-strength-of-ak-lng-in-report/59901/ |access-date=2024-10-01 |website=Alaska Native News |language=en-US}}</ref>

In 2022, Alaska officials, including Dunleavy, the head of the state's gas line agency, and [[Dan Sullivan (U.S. senator)|U.S. Senator Dan Sullivan]], met with Japanese energy representatives to promote the Alaska LNG project in [[Tokyo]]. U.S. Ambassador to Japan [[Rahm Emanuel]] convened the meeting to discuss the prospect of exporting liquefied natural gas from Alaska's North Slope to [[Japan]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=DeMarban |first=Alex |date=October 25, 2022 |title=LNG summit in Japan promotes Alaska's $39 billion North Slope gas project |url=https://www.adn.com/business-economy/energy/2022/10/25/lng-summit-in-japan-promotes-alaskas-39-billion-north-slope-gas-project/ |access-date=2024-10-01 |website=Anchorage Daily News |language=en}}</ref>

In 2024, an independent report by [[Wood Mackenzie]] estimated that the pipeline project could stimulate industrial demand, reduce emissions, and provide long-term economic benefits to Alaska. The report also concluded that the delivered cost of gas via the pipeline would likely be lower than LNG imports, especially when considering the potential for additional industrial demand.

In March 2025, Dunleavy announced<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Thompson |first=John |date=2025-03-28 |title=Dunleavy calls in from Tokyo for international press conference on LNG developments |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/03/28/dunleavy-calls-tokyo-international-press-conference-lng-developments/? |access-date=2025-03-28 |website=Alaska News Source |language=en}}</ref> significant progress on the Alaska LNG Project to transport natural gas from the [[North Slope Borough, Alaska|North Slope]] to global markets. The state reached a definitive agreement with Glenfarne Group,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nikše |first=Dragana |date=2025-03-28 |title=US player tasked with taking Alaskan LNG project to finish line |url=https://www.offshore-energy.biz/us-player-tasked-with-taking-alaskan-lng-project-to-finish-line/? |access-date=2025-03-28 |website=Offshore Energy |language=en-US}}</ref> a private energy infrastructure firm, granting it a 75% ownership stake in 8 Star Alaska—the entity managing the project. Glenfarne will lead the development through to construction and operation, with the [[Alaska|State of Alaska]] retaining a 25% share. The project encompasses an 807-mile pipeline, a liquefaction facility in [[Nikiski, Alaska|Nikiski]] capable of exporting up to 20 million tons of LNG annually, and a [[Carbon capture and storage|carbon capture]] plant designed to sequester 7 million tons of [[carbon dioxide]] each year. During a press conference in Tokyo,<ref name=":0" /> part of a broader Asian tour to secure investment and buyers,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Houston |first=Timothy W. Martin in Seoul and Benoît Morenne in |title=The Frontman Selling Trump's Largest Energy Project to Nervous U.S. Allies |url=https://www.wsj.com/world/asia/trump-lng-pipeline-mike-dunleavy-alaska-governor-32858e9d? |access-date=2025-03-28 |website=WSJ |language=en-US}}</ref> Dunleavy highlighted a non-binding letter of intent from [[Taiwan]]'s [[CPC Corporation]] to purchase 6 million tons of LNG annually and potentially invest in the project. Discussions with officials and business leaders in [[South Korea]] and [[Japan]] also indicated interest, though no binding agreements were reached. Dunleavy emphasized the project's potential to enhance energy security for both Alaska and its Asian partners, aiming for a final investment decision by the end of 2025 and operations to commence by 2030.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dye |first=Jacob |date=2025-03-26 |title=Glenfarne takes majority stake of Alaska LNG Project, will lead development |url=https://www.juneauempire.com/news/glenfarne-takes-majority-stake-of-alaska-lng-project-will-lead-development/? |access-date=2025-03-28 |website=Juneau Empire |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Education ===

==== Alaska Reads Act ====
{{main|Alaska Reads Act}}
[[File:GV1_2459.jpg|left|frameless|Governor Mike Dunleavy during the ceremony celebrating the Alaska Reads Act signed into law]]
In 2020, Dunleavy announced a bipartisan initiative called the Alaska Reads Act,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Osborne |first=Natalie |date=2020-01-16 |title=Governor Dunleavy Unveils Alaska Reads Act |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-unveils-alaska-reads-act/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref> which "focused on enhancing interventions for struggling readers and offering targeted school-improvement in the state's lowest performing schools". The Alaska Reads Act aimed to improve reading for kindergarten through third-grade students with a reading intervention program, teacher training, and data reporting requirements. Dunleavy worked with State Senator [[Tom Begich]] to introduce the bill. The [[Alaska Legislature]] passed the Alaska Reads Act in May 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Midvag |first=George |date=2022-06-08 |title=Governor Dunleavy, Legislators Celebrate Successful Education Legislation |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-legislators-celebrate-successful-education-legislation/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Anastas |first=Katie |date=2022-06-08 |title=Gov. Dunleavy, lawmakers celebrate passage of Alaska Reads Act |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2022/06/08/gov-dunleavy-lawmakers-celebrate-passage-of-alaska-reads-act/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref> Implementation of the program began in 2023. Program outcomes in 2024 showed improvement. At the beginning of the school year, 41% of students reached early literacy benchmarks. By the end of the year, this figure increased to 57%. Among kindergartners, the proficiency rate rose from 24% at the start of the year to 60% by the year's end.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Reading scores improving |url=https://www.kvakradio.com/2024/06/07/reading-scores-improving/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |website=KVAK Valdez, Alaska. |language=en-US}}</ref> Dunleavy issued the statement, "I'm encouraged by the improvements Alaska's students are already experiencing because of the Alaska Reads Act, as these results are beginning to show, when we implement effective education reform, Alaska's students are capable of success."<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-06-07 |title=Alaska Sees Boost In Early Literacy Following Alaska Reads Act - Radio Kenai |url=https://radiokenai.com/alaska-sees-boost-in-early-literacy-following-alaska-reads-act/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Schroeder |first=Kollette |date=2024-06-05 |title=Alaska Realizes Boost in Early Literacy Following Alaska Reads Act Implementation |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/alaska-realizes-boost-in-early-literacy-following-alaska-reads-act-implementation/ |access-date=2024-06-10 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== COVID-19 ===
{{See also|COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic in Alaska}}
On March 11, 2020, Dunleavy's office declared a state of emergency to ensure all entities had the necessary response resources a day before the first case arrived via a foreign national in [[Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Issues Public Health Disaster Emergency Declaration for COVID-19 – Mike Dunleavy |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2020/03/11/governor-issues-public-health-disaster-emergency-declaration-for-covid-19/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=gov.alaska.gov |language=en-US |archive-date=April 11, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200411085200/https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2020/03/11/governor-issues-public-health-disaster-emergency-declaration-for-covid-19/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Two days later, Dunleavy ordered public schools to close from March 16 to 30.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 15, 2020 |title=Alaska officials close public schools to students through March 30 to limit spread of coronavirus |url=https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/2020/03/14/alaska-officials-close-k-12-public-schools-to-students-for-extra-2-weeks-to-limit-spread-of-coronavirus/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=Anchorage Daily News |language=en |archive-date=April 11, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200411085138/https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/2020/03/14/alaska-officials-close-k-12-public-schools-to-students-for-extra-2-weeks-to-limit-spread-of-coronavirus/ |url-status=live }}</ref> That April, Dunleavy activated the State Emergency Operations Center under Alaska's Department of Military and Veterans Affairs. [[Joint Task Force-Alaska]] was stood up to provide a coordinated effort for the [[Alaska Army National Guard|Alaska Army]] and [[Alaska Air National Guard|Air National Guard]], the [[Alaska State Defense Force]], and the [[Alaska Naval Militia]] to support the state.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Proactive Posture and Partnership The formation of Joint Task Force-Alaska and its preparation to help tackle COVID-19 |url=https://www.dvidshub.net/news/367766/proactive-posture-and-partnership-formation-joint-task-force-alaska-and-its-preparation-help-tackle-covid-19 |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=DVIDS |language=en |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://www.dvidshub.net/news/367766/proactive-posture-and-partnership-formation-joint-task-force-alaska-and-its-preparation-help-tackle-covid-19 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On May 19, Dunleavy announced the lifting of all state mandates for businesses and public gatherings, keeping only a mandatory (but unenforced) quarantine period for persons coming from out of state.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hanlon |first=Tegan |date=2020-05-20 |title='We're open': Alaska businesses can operate at full capacity on Friday, Dunleavy says |url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2020/05/19/were-open-alaska-businesses-can-fully-open-on-friday-dunleavy-says/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2020/05/19/were-open-alaska-businesses-can-fully-open-on-friday-dunleavy-says/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A month later, Dunleavy announced a new extension of the two-week quarantine measure that required visitors to Alaska to present a negative test for the virus if they were not willing to self-quarantine for two weeks.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meek |first=Andy |date=2020-06-07 |title=You may have to provide a negative coronavirus test before you visit Alaska |url=https://bgr.com/lifestyle/coronavirus-us-alaska-visitors-covid-19-test-quarantine/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=BGR |language=en-US |archive-date=July 30, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210730175315/https://bgr.com/lifestyle/coronavirus-us-alaska-visitors-covid-19-test-quarantine/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In April 2021, Dunleavy announced that Alaska would offer free vaccinations to tourists at major airports starting June 1, as part of the United States' vaccination campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bohrer |first=Becky |title=Alaska to offer tourists COVID-19 vaccines at major airports starting June 1 |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/travel/news/2021/04/17/alaska-offer-tourists-covid-19-vaccines-starting-june-1/7268353002/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=USA TODAY |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://www.usatoday.com/story/travel/news/2021/04/17/alaska-offer-tourists-covid-19-vaccines-starting-june-1/7268353002/ |url-status=live }}</ref> That October, Dunleavy refused to issue another COVID-19 disaster declaration, thinking it unnecessary. "Exercising the Disaster Act does not give our team any more health tools than what they need and are using right now", he said, adding, "Masking is, as I have stated, a local issue best left to local leaders."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maguire |first=Sean |title=Alaska governor again rejects calls to issue another COVID-19 disaster declaration |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2021/10/08/alaska-governor-again-rejects-calls-issue-another-covid-19-disaster-declaration/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=alaskasnewssource.com |date=October 8, 2021 |language=en |archive-date=October 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008233912/https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2021/10/08/alaska-governor-again-rejects-calls-issue-another-covid-19-disaster-declaration/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Dunleavy later clarified that he didn't endorse mask or vaccine mandates but wouldn't ban them either.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Herz |first=Nathaniel |date=2021-10-15 |title=Alaska Gov. Dunleavy won't endorse mask or vaccine mandates. But he says he won't ban them either. |url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2021/10/15/alaska-gov-dunleavy-wont-endorse-mask-or-vaccine-mandates-but-he-says-he-wont-ban-them-either/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194117/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2021/10/15/alaska-gov-dunleavy-wont-endorse-mask-or-vaccine-mandates-but-he-says-he-wont-ban-them-either/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The next month, he ordered state agencies to ignore federal vaccine mandates, arguing that they were "unconstitutional" and "completely unnecessary" and claiming that Alaska had handled COVID better than nearly every other state.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mole |first=Beth |date=2021-11-03 |title=Alaska Governor orders state agencies to ignore federal vaccine mandates |url=https://arstechnica.com/science/2021/11/despite-sky-high-case-rates-alaska-governor-wages-war-on-vaccine-mandates/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=Ars Technica |language=en-us |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194113/https://arstechnica.com/science/2021/11/despite-sky-high-case-rates-alaska-governor-wages-war-on-vaccine-mandates/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Abortion ===
Dunleavy opposes abortion. In July 2019, he vetoed $334,700 for the state to pay for abortions from the court system's budget. The Dunleavy administration wrote, "The legislative and executive branch are opposed to state-funded elective abortions; the only branch of government that insists on state-funded elective abortions is the Supreme Court."<ref>{{Cite web |first=Nat |last=Herz |date=2019-07-01 |title=Dunleavy wields veto pen to attack state Supreme Court over abortion ruling |url=https://www.ktoo.org/2019/07/01/dunleavy-wields-veto-pen-to-attack-state-supreme-court-over-abortion-ruling/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=KTOO |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526195024/https://www.ktoo.org/2019/07/01/dunleavy-wields-veto-pen-to-attack-state-supreme-court-over-abortion-ruling/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A year later, Anchorage Superior Court Judge [[Jennifer S. Henderson]] ruled that Dunleavy's vetoes in 2019 and 2020 were unconstitutional and violated the separation of powers doctrine: "In spite of this Court's faith that the Alaska judiciary remains independent and committed to its essential function of deciding cases according to the rule of law, the Court must unfortunately conclude that in vetoing funds appropriated to the State appellate courts in express retaliation against the Alaska Supreme Court for its legal decision-making, the Governor violated the separation of powers doctrine."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kitchenman |first=Andrew |date=2020-10-16 |title=Dunleavy's court system vetoes because of abortion funding were illegal, judge says |url=https://www.alaskapublic.org/2020/10/16/dunleavys-court-system-vetoes-because-of-abortion-funding-were-illegal-judge-says/ |access-date=2022-04-28 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194115/https://www.alaskapublic.org/2020/10/16/dunleavys-court-system-vetoes-because-of-abortion-funding-were-illegal-judge-says/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Alaska Permanent Fund Dividend===
As a candidate<ref>{{Cite web |last=Resneck |first=Jacob |date=2018-12-03 |title=PFD promise key to Dunleavy's win in Hoonah |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2018/12/03/pfd-promise-key-to-dunleavys-win-in-hoonah/ |access-date=2024-06-27 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref> and governor, Dunleavy advocated for the [[Alaska Permanent Fund Dividend]] (PFD), consistently pushing for full statutory payments to Alaskans.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-01-08 |title=Dunleavy vows to crack down on crime, restore PFD |url=https://www.juneauempire.com/news/dunleavy-vows-to-crack-down-on-crime-restore-pfd/ |access-date=2024-06-27 |website=Juneau Empire |language=en-US}}</ref> The Alaska Permanent Fund is a state-owned [[investment fund]] established in 1976 to invest [[Surplus value|surplus]] revenue from Alaska's [[Fossil fuel|oil and gas]] reserves. The PFD is an annual payout from the fund's earnings to eligible Alaska residents. During Governor [[Bill Walker (American politician)|Bill Walker]]'s administration, the payments were reduced through the legislative budget process.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Buxton |first=Matt |date=2016-06-29 |title=Walker caps PFD at $1,000 amid $1.29 billion in budget vetoes |url=https://www.newsminer.com/news/alaska_news/walker-caps-pfd-at-1-000-amid-1-29-billion-in-budget-vetoes/article_b1d766c4-3e20-11e6-8f35-ef28d02a7a39.html |access-date=2024-06-27 |website=Fairbanks Daily News-Miner |language=en}}</ref> A fundamental change in how the payouts were conducted resulted in smaller checks.

In 2019, Dunleavy introduced bills to restore the dividends and repay Alaskans for the previous reduced payments, saying, "The PFD must be paid out according to the law, Alaskans must be paid back what the governor arbitrarily took from them, and future actions involving the PFD must be approved by voters."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Osborne |first=Natalie |date=2019-01-18 |title=Governor Dunleavy Unveils PFD Back Pay Legislation |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-unveils-pfd-back-pay-legislation/ |access-date=2024-06-27 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref> In 2020, he submitted a budget amendment to the legislature to fully fund the 2019 dividend.

Dunleavy also submitted bills to establish a PFD Land [[Voucher]] program to allow Alaska residents to receive land vouchers worth twice the value of a dividend for purchasing state land. This initiative aimed to promote land ownership among residents and enhance state revenue.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Back |first=Trevor |date=March 10, 2020 |title=Alaska Wants Residents to Invest in Land |url=https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/articles/2020-03-10/alaska-gov-dunleavy-suggests-land-vouchers-instead-of-permanent-fund-dividends |access-date=June 27, 2024 |work=US News & World Report}}</ref>

Despite legislative resistance, Dunleavy continued to advocate higher dividends. In 2021, he proposed an amendment to the [[Constitution of Alaska|Alaska Constitution]] to guarantee the dividend's payment by using a formula.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-05-13 |title=Alaska governor proposes dividend, oil-wealth fund plan |url=https://apnews.com/article/ak-state-wire-alaska-philanthropy-government-and-politics-4ab2d1bf806e504625932b84e7fa92fe |access-date=2024-06-27 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> By 2022, with oil revenues rising to unexpected levels, a $3,700 PFD was proposed to give immediate economic relief to Alaskans facing high inflation and fuel costs. Revenue forecasts suggested that the state could afford significant PFD payments while maintaining budget surpluses and savings.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kitchenman |first=Andrew |date=2022-02-18 |title=As oil prices and inflation rise, Dunleavy pushes for higher PFDs and bonds |url=https://www.ktoo.org/2022/02/17/as-oil-prices-and-inflation-rise-gov-dunleavy-pushes-for-higher-pfds-and-bonds/ |access-date=2024-06-27 |website=KTOO |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Environment ===
Dunleavy rejects the [[scientific consensus on climate change]]. In February 2019, he abolished Alaska's climate change task force, a team instated by [[Bill Walker (American politician)|Bill Walker]], calling it unnecessary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rosen |first=Yereth |date=2019-02-26 |title=Alaska's new governor has dismissed the state's climate team and scrapped its climate policy and plan |url=https://www.arctictoday.com/alaskas-new-governor-has-dismissed-the-states-climate-team-and-scrapped-climate-policy-and-plan/ |access-date=2022-05-22 |website=ArcticToday |language=en-US |archive-date=January 27, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220127141518/https://www.arctictoday.com/alaskas-new-governor-has-dismissed-the-states-climate-team-and-scrapped-climate-policy-and-plan/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In September 2019, during a meeting at the [[International Forum of Sovereign Wealth Funds]] with [[Mark Gordon]], Dunleavy said that warming the Arctic could be good for Alaska, believing that it could create further business opportunities.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hohenstatt |first=Ben |date=2019-09-12 |title=Gov says warming Arctic could be good for Alaska |url=https://www.juneauempire.com/news/gov-says-warming-arctic-could-be-good-for-alaska/ |access-date=2022-05-22 |website=Juneau Empire |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://www.juneauempire.com/news/gov-says-warming-arctic-could-be-good-for-alaska/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In October 2019, Dunleavy clashed with Representative [[Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez]] on Twitter over the [[Green New Deal]] proposal, saying that the Green New Deal would impact our civilization as we know it.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Buxton |first=Matt Acuña |date=2019-10-30 |title=Dunleavy, AOC spar over climate change, Green New Deal on Twitter |url=https://midnightsunak.com/2019/10/29/dunleavy-aoc-spar-over-climate-change-green-new-deal-on-twitter/ |access-date=2022-05-22 |website=The Midnight Sun |language=en-US |archive-date=March 5, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210305192543/https://midnightsunak.com/2019/10/29/dunleavy-aoc-spar-over-climate-change-green-new-deal-on-twitter/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

=== Energy and natural resources ===
In September 2020, Dunleavy expressed support for [[renewable energy]] ideas: "I know there's a view on the part of some that a Republican governor that is supportive of Alaska's resource extraction industries, including those around fossil fuels, would not want anything to do with renewables" and "It makes total sense to explore pumped hydro, using wind as a main source of energy and the reservoir as the batteries."<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-09-23 |title=Pro-oil Alaska governor also backs renewable energy ideas |url=https://apnews.com/article/climate-climate-change-anchorage-alaska-archive-0516a343c522ec1b1912a4f1c119ae37 |access-date=2022-05-22 |website=AP NEWS |language=en |archive-date= May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://apnews.com/article/climate-climate-change-anchorage-alaska-archive-0516a343c522ec1b1912a4f1c119ae37 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In February 2022, Dunleavy denounced the Biden administration's request for suspension of the [[Ambler Road]] Project: "The Biden Administration has opened yet another front in its war on Alaska. You would think President Biden would want to improve access to American sources of copper and other strategic minerals that are needed in our combined efforts to increase renewables. Instead, actions like this only serve to push development to Third World nations that don't have the environmental ethic that Alaskans have. This pendulum swing away from the last federal administration's approval disregards extensive environmental studies and widespread social engagement while creating instability in long-term investment."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Dunleavy Denounces President Biden's Obstruction of Ambler Road – Mike Dunleavy |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2022/02/23/governor-dunleavy-denounces-president-bidens-obstruction-of-ambler-road/ |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=gov.alaska.gov |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194115/https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2022/02/23/governor-dunleavy-denounces-president-bidens-obstruction-of-ambler-road/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Dunleavy has encouraged the [[United States Environmental Protection Agency]] to approve the permit for [[Pebble Mine]], which other Alaska politicians oppose because it would threaten the fishery of [[Bristol Bay]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=DeMarban |first=Alex |date=25 October 2022 |title=Alaska Gov. Dunleavy urges EPA to stop veto of Pebble mine |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2022/10/25/alaska-gov-dunleavy-urges-epa-to-stop-veto-of-pebble-mine/ |access-date=2022-11-08 |website=Anchorage Daily News |language=en}}</ref>

==== Alaska Sustainable Energy Conference ====
In May 2022, Dunleavy sponsored the first annual Alaska Sustainable Energy Conference in Anchorage. Over 80 speakers, including policymakers and experts, discussed strategies to harness Alaska's renewable energy resources and achieve energy independence. Over 500 people attended.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-06-02 |title=Governor Dunleavy Concludes Successful First Annual Sustainable Energy Conference |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-concludes-successful-first-annual-sustainable-energy-conference/ |access-date=2024-06-11 |website=State of Alaska |language=en-US}}</ref> On the conference's first day, Dunleavy signed into law a [[nuclear microreactor]] bill<ref>{{Cite web |title=Senate Bill 177 |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Bill/Detail/32?Root=sb%20177 |access-date=2024-06-11 |website=Alaska State Legislature}}</ref> intended to create low-cost, reliable power for communities, remote villages, and resource development projects, and reduce reliance on volatile energy sources.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rosen |first=Yereth |date=2022 |title=Governor signs bill intended to encourage nuclear microreactors in Alaska • Alaska Beacon |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/briefs/governor-signs-microreactor-bill/ |access-date=June 11, 2024 |website=Alaska Beacon |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Carbon offset markets ====
In 2023, Dunleavy introduced a legislative package<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-01-13 |title=Alaska governor pitches plan to capitalize on carbon markets |url=https://apnews.com/article/alaska-state-government-business-d5c9bed95c03b594884448f94d75d3e4 |access-date=2024-06-13 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> to diversify Alaska's revenue through carbon markets alongside existing oil and gas revenues and the [[Alaska Permanent Fund]]. The legislation, signed into law on May 23,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Turner |first=Jeff |date=2023-05-23 |title=Governor Dunleavy Signs Carbon Offset Legislation |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-signs-carbon-offset-legislation/ |access-date=2024-06-13 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref> established rules for storing carbon dioxide underground and initiated a carbon offsets program. This initiative aimed to complement existing industries, not replace them.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Canny |first=Anna Canny |date=2023-01-13 |title=New bill from Dunleavy aims to help Alaska break into growing carbon markets |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2023/01/13/new-bill-from-dunleavy-aims-to-help-alaska-break-into-growing-carbon-markets/ |access-date=2024-06-13 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref>

"This bill specifically creates new authorities for State agencies to license, lease, and administer the State's pore space for geological storage; administer pipeline infrastructure for transportation of captured carbon to geological storage facilities and administer injection wells and carbon storage facilities; and protect correlative rights of all subsurface owners."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Turner |first=Jeff |date=2023-01-28 |title=Governor Dunleavy Introduces Carbon Management and Monetization Bills Creating Statutory Structures |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-introduces-carbon-management-and-monetization-bills-creating-statutory-structures/ |access-date=2024-06-13 |website=State of Alaska |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Unlocking Alaska Initiative ====
[[File:Governor_Dunleavy_with_President_Donald_Trump.jpg|thumb|Governor Dunleavy with President Donald Trump]]
Dunleavy's administration took several initiatives to assert state control over federal lands and waters. These efforts aimed to reduce federal overreach and increase Alaska's autonomy in managing its [[natural resource]]s. In March 2021, Dunleavy announced the "[https://gov.alaska.gov/unlocking-alaska/ Unlocking Alaska]" initiative to assert state control over approximately 800,000 miles of Alaska's [[Navigable Waters of the United States|navigable]] rivers, lakes, and tidelands. This move aimed to end decades of federal obstruction and delay in recognizing Alaska's ownership of these waters, and to enforce [[States' rights|state rights]] promised under the [[Alaska Statehood Act]]. The initiative was inspired by the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] decision [[Sturgeon v. Frost]], which affirmed Alaska's authority over its navigable waters within federal conservation units. Dunleavy emphasized reducing federal interference and enhancing state management for economic use and recreational access. He took legal and administrative actions, including a [[lawsuit]] against the federal government, issuing trespass notices, and sending cease-and-desist letters to federal agencies such as the [[United States Forest Service|Forest Service]] and the [[National Park Service]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ruskin |first=Liz |date=2022-04-27 |title=Dunleavy gets after feds to recognize Alaska's ownership of submerged lands |url=https://alaskapublic.org/2022/04/26/dunleavy-gets-after-feds-to-recognize-alaskas-ownership-of-submerged-lands/ |access-date=2024-07-02 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Maguire |first=Sean |date=2021-03-27 |title=Governor asserts state sovereignty over Alaska's navigable rivers, lakes and tidelands |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2021/03/27/governor-asserts-state-sovereignty-over-800000-miles-of-alaskas-rivers-lakes-and-tidelands/ |access-date=2024-07-02 |website=Alaska News Source |language=en}}</ref>

Dunleavy's "Unlocking Alaska" initiative was boosted when the [[Interior Board of Land Appeals]] upheld Alaska's ownership of 7,552 acres of submerged lands under the [[Kuskokwim River]]. This decision validated the state's long-standing claims under the [[Equal footing|Equal Footing Doctrine]] and the [[Submerged Lands Act]]. The state had initially applied for a recordable disclaimer of interest, which the [[Bureau of Land Management]] denied. The appeal's success supported further actions by Alaska to assert land rights and challenge federal overreach. "The essential legal principles supporting Alaska's win in this case are the same ones underlying my 'Unlocking Alaska' Initiative", Dunleavy said. "While it's taken nearly six years, the Interior Department has acknowledged that the U.S. Constitution and federal law make it clear Alaska owns submerged lands in Alaska."<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-09-22 |title=Win in Federal Land Appeal Bolsters "Unlocking Alaska" Initiative |url=https://www.akbizmag.com/industry/government/win-in-federal-land-appeal-bolsters-unlocking-alaska-initiative/ |access-date=2024-07-02 |website=Alaska Business Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Criminal justice ===
From 2019 to 2023, Alaska's crime rate dropped about 31%. During the same period, violent crime dropped by 15.9%.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 2024 |title=Crime in Alaska 2023 |url=https://dps.alaska.gov/getmedia/e172226d-cf30-45c7-a8d3-63560d635b8f/Crime-in-Alaska-2023 |website=Criminal Records & Identification Bureau}}</ref>

==== Repeal of SB91 and other initiatives ====
Dunleavy's campaign platform focused on repealing and replacing Senate Bill 91 (SB91),<ref>{{Cite web |title=SB91 - Alaska State Legislature |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/Bill/Detail/29?Root=SB91 |access-date=2024-06-17 |website=www.akleg.gov}}</ref> a comprehensive criminal justice reform act signed into law by Governor [[Bill Walker (American politician)|Bill Walker]] on July 11, 2016. SB91 implemented recommendations from a 2015 Alaska Criminal Justice Commission report<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 2015 |title=Justice Reinvestment Report |url=https://www.akleg.gov/basis/get_documents.asp?session=29&docid=40449 |access-date=17 June 2024 |website=Alaska Criminal Justice Commission: Justice Reinvestment Report}}</ref> to reform Alaska's parole and pretrial systems, generally lowering criminal punishments for nonviolent offenses. But the implementation of SB91 coincided with a spike in crime, leading to concern and anger among many Alaskans. This rise in crime was largely attributed to the reforms SB91 introduced.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kitchenman |first1=Andrew |date=2017-10-05 |title=As anger over crime boils over, Alaska lawmakers weigh changes to law |url=https://www.ktoo.org/2017/10/04/anger-crime-boils-lawmakers-weigh-changes-law/ |access-date=2024-06-17 |website=KTOO |language=en-US}}</ref>
[[File:GV1_0225.jpg|thumb|Governor Mike Dunleavy during the bill signing ceremony for HB49]]
In January 2019, Dunleavy announced that he would repeal and replace SB91<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maguire |first=Sean |date=2019-01-24 |title=Gov. Dunleavy calls for repeal of SB91 |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/content/news/Dunleavy-calls-for-stiff-sentences-and-repeal-of-SB91-Alaska-legislature-504786111.html |access-date=2024-06-17 |website=Alaska News Source |language=en}}</ref> and declare "war on criminals" by proposing four bills that would increase criminal penalties for sexual offenses; reverse a range of reductions to sentences; add a new category of crime called terroristic threatening; increase [[bail]] and give judges more discretion in how people charged with crimes are released before trials; and reduce the use of [[parole]]. He said, "If you are a criminal, this is the beginning of the end for your activities", and "If you're going to assault people—if you're going to engage in sexual assaults, physical assaults—this is going to be a very unsafe place for you. We're not going to tolerate it at all."<ref>{{Cite web |first=Andrew |last=Kitchenman |date=2019-01-24 |title=Dunleavy rolls out package in first step of 'war on criminals' |url=https://www.ktoo.org/2019/01/23/dunleavy-rolls-out-package-in-first-step-of-war-on-criminals/ |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=KTOO |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://www.ktoo.org/2019/01/23/dunleavy-rolls-out-package-in-first-step-of-war-on-criminals/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Dunleavy Introduces Legislation Addressing Crime in Alaska – Mike Dunleavy |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2022/02/11/governor-dunleavy-introduces-legislation-addressing-crime-in-alaska/ |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=gov.alaska.gov |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2022/02/11/governor-dunleavy-introduces-legislation-addressing-crime-in-alaska/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> One of these bills, House Bill 49 (HB49), passed the legislature and repealed many reforms made in 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maguire |first=Sean |date=2019-05-29 |title=Tough-on-crime bill passes through the Legislature, will be signed by Gov. Dunleavy |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/content/news/Tough-on-crime-bill-passes-through-the-Legislature-will-be-signed-by-Gov-Dunleavy-510540801.html |access-date=2024-06-17 |website=Alaska News Source |language=en}}</ref> On Twitter, Dunleavy posted, "Public safety has been priority # 1 for my admin, I'm pleased the House has passed legislation to repeal the failed SB91 & replace it with common sense crime laws that will give police, judges, & prosecutors the tools they need to address the crime wave in Alaska." He signed HB49 into law on July 8, 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Brooks |first=James |date=8 July 2019 |title=Gov. Dunleavy signs legislation to repeal, replace the crime-reform measure SB 91 |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/alaska-legislature/2019/07/09/gov-dunleavy-signs-legislation-to-repeal-replace-the-crime-reform-measure-sb-91/ |access-date=2024-06-17 |website=Anchorage Daily News |language=en}}</ref>

In 2024, Dunleavy signed a comprehensive crime bill aimed at improving public safety. The legislation targets issues like [[fentanyl]], child exploitation, and [[human trafficking]], while enhancing protections for crime victims. It includes stricter penalties for drug-related deaths, updates the [[Sex offender registries in the United States|sex offender registry]], and introduces new crimes, such as assaulting someone in a child's presence.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schroeder |first=Kollette |date=2024-07-11 |title=Governor Mike Dunleavy Signs Landmark Omnibus Bill to Combat Crime and Protect Crime Victims |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-mike-dunleavy-signs-landmark-omnibus-bill-to-combat-crime-and-protect-crime-victims/ |access-date=2024-10-16 |website=State of Alaska |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Public safety ===

==== Staffing and vacancies ====
From the start of his tenure, Dunleavy's administration, along with the [[Alaska State Legislature|legislature]], steadily increased the number of budgeted [[law enforcement]] positions, reflecting a commitment to strengthening public safety. In [[fiscal year]] 2019, the state allocated funding for 387 positions, which grew each year, reaching 444 budgeted positions by fiscal year 2025—a 14.7% increase over the period.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Budget Reports – Office of Management and Budget |url=https://omb.alaska.gov/budget-reports/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250305003452/https://omb.alaska.gov/budget-reports/ |archive-date=2025-03-05 |access-date=2025-03-21 |website=omb.alaska.gov |language=en-US}}</ref>

====Sexual assault rape kits====
In 2021, Dunleavy's administration announced that thousands of unprocessed sexual assault kits had been cleared. In 2016, it was discovered that there was a significant backlog of SAR kits. Dunleavy said, "We've put in place internal steps to prevent this kind of backlog from ever happening again and added resources to the State crime lab to ensure every kit is tested within 90 days or sooner."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Enslow |first1=Patrick |title=State of Alaska announces thousands of unprocessed sexual assault kits have been cleared |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2021/12/15/state-alaska-announces-thousands-unprocessed-sexual-assault-kits-have-been-cleared/ |access-date=21 June 2024 |agency=Gray Media Group |publisher=Alaska's News Source |date=14 December 2021}}</ref> His initiative included the use of software and a tracking database to allow survivors and agencies involved in sexual assault response to track the status and location of sexual assault examination kits.<ref>{{cite web |title=Backlog Cleared, Statewide, in Sexual Assault Examination Kits |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/backlog-cleared-statewide-in-sexual-assault-examination-kits/ |website=State of Alaska |date=December 21, 2021 |access-date=21 June 2024}}</ref>

==== George Floyd ====
In May 2020, after the murder of [[George Floyd]], Dunleavy called the act "horrific" and thanked Alaskans for their peaceful protests. "People absolutely have the right to protest: This is America", Dunleavy said. "What occurred in Minneapolis when Mr. Floyd was killed in that police action, we all know is terrible." But Dunleavy expressed concern that the protests in Alaska could spread [[COVID-19]], noting that any events that combine people being in close proximity with singing and shouting can spread the virus. He emphasized advice from state officials that people at gatherings wear face masks or stay at least 6 to 10 feet away from others.<ref>{{Cite web |first=Andrew |last=Kitchenman |date=2020-06-04 |title=Dunleavy calls George Floyd's death 'horrific,' thanks Alaskans for peaceful protests |url=https://www.ktoo.org/2020/06/03/dunleavy-calls-george-floyds-death-horrific-thanks-alaskans-for-peaceful-protests/ |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=KTOO |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194115/https://www.ktoo.org/2020/06/03/dunleavy-calls-george-floyds-death-horrific-thanks-alaskans-for-peaceful-protests/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Second Amendment ===
Dunleavy advocated for Alaskans' [[Second Amendment to the United States Constitution|Second Amendment]] right to bear arms while campaigning and during his tenure. He was endorsed by the [[NRA Political Victory Fund]] in 2018<ref>{{Cite web |last=NRA-PVF |title=NRA Endorses Mike Dunleavy for Governor of Alaska |url=https://www.nrapvf.org/articles/20181101/nra-endorses-mike-dunleavy-for-governor-of-alaska |access-date=2025-01-18 |website=NRA-PVF |language=en}}</ref> and 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=NRA-PVF |title=NRA-PVF Endorses Gov. Dunleavy in Alaska Primary |url=https://www.nrapvf.org/articles/20220719/nra-pvf-endorses-gov-dunleavy-in-alaska-primary |access-date=2025-01-18 |website=NRA-PVF |language=en}}</ref>

In 2022, Dunleavy applauded a [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] decision that struck down [[New York (state)|New York]]'s firearm licensing policy as [[Constitutionality|unconstitutional]], calling it a significant win for the Second Amendment and law-abiding Americans.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Midvag |first=George |date=2022-06-23 |title=Governor Dunleavy Applauds Today's Supreme Court Decision Protecting Second Amendment Rights |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/governor-dunleavy-applauds-todays-supreme-court-decision-protecting-second-amendment-rights/ |access-date=2024-07-23 |website=Mike Dunleavy |language=en-US}}</ref>

In 2023, he supported and signed into law House Bill 61, a bill to block state and local officials from closing gun stores during disasters declared by the governor unless such closures apply to all other businesses. This marked the first major pro-Second Amendment legislation passed in Alaska. "This bill reflects the constitutional right Alaskans have to keep and bear arms by protecting access to both firearms and ammunition when Alaskans need it the most", Dunleavy said.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Morris |first=Kyle |date=2023-07-29 |title=Second Amendment advocates celebrate Alaska Gov. Dunleavy signing gun store law: 'A protective shield' |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/second-amendment-advocates-celebrate-alaska-gov-dunleavy-signing-gun-store-law-protective-shield |access-date=2024-07-23 |website=Fox News |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Immigration ===
In May 2022, Dunleavy expressed opposition to the termination of [[Title 42 expulsion|Title 42]] by the [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention|Centers for Disease Control]]: "The Termination Order is detrimental to the states tasked with enforcing immigration standards, and it is not logically appropriate" and "This policy runs contrary to the Biden Administration's other declarations because it is expressly premised on the decrease of COVID-19, but the Administration has ignored these facts by enforcing mandatory vaccination and mask mandates."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Alaska Opposes Termination of Title 42 by the CDC – Mike Dunleavy |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2022/04/14/alaska-opposes-termination-of-title-42-by-the-cdc/ |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=gov.alaska.gov |language=en-US |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194115/https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2022/04/14/alaska-opposes-termination-of-title-42-by-the-cdc/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

=== Voting rights ===
In January 2022, Dunleavy called on legislators to "improve election integrity" by prohibiting [[automatic voter registration]], tracking [[absentee balloting]], requiring signature verification, and implementing voter roll maintenance, saying, "We just want to make sure that as we move forward in Alaska that our concerns, our worries, are taken care of."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maguire |first=Sean |title=Dunleavy administration makes renewed election integrity legislation push |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2022/01/21/dunleavy-administration-makes-renewed-election-integrity-legislation-push/ |access-date=2022-05-26 |website=alaskasnewssource.com |date=January 21, 2022 |language=en |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526194114/https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2022/01/21/dunleavy-administration-makes-renewed-election-integrity-legislation-push/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

